# Build the caster up to Mage 5, put a Staff of the Abyss in his weapon hand,
# and photograph three things: his class level, his alignment, and every stati
# he holds. Read by tools/check_staff_abyss_alignment.sh, finding PA-08-F8 of
# bd inc-tek.8.8.
#
# This file is the half that is the SAME for both alignments. Two sessions
# have to run, because nothing in wizard mode can change a character's
# alignment after generation -- the menu (src/Debug.cpp:534-572) has no such
# option -- so the two halves of the finding need two characters. The two
# callers differ only in the alignment they pick during generation:
#
#   tools/keys/staff-abyss-alignment-good.keys      Neutral Good
#   tools/keys/staff-abyss-alignment-nongood.keys   Neutral
#
# WHY MAGE 5. The staff's own EV_MAGIC_HIT handler calls
# ItemPrereq(CLEV_VAL + SP_ARCANE + SP_DIVINE,5,20) and returns DONE -- which
# swallows the event before Magic::Grant runs -- when that fails. ItemPrereq's
# CLEV_VAL branch (src/Skills.cpp:5681-5692) sums the highest SPELL_ACCESS
# magnitude per casting type, so a Mage 5 reads 5 and a Mage 4 reads 4. Below
# five the staff is silent for EVERY alignment, and a session built that way
# would show the good character empty-handed for the wrong reason. So the
# check refuses to believe anything unless the sheet says Mage 5.
#
# HOW THE LEVELS ARE GAINED. Wizard mode's "Free Advancement" switch lets the
# character sheet's [G]ain Level work without the experience
# (src/Managers.cpp:2221). Each mage level opens up to three menus of its own,
# which cover the sheet, so the level is gained one G at a time and each G is
# followed by the menus it can raise.
#
# THE ORACLE is wizard mode's "Examine Player Data", which prints every stati
# with its Val and its Mag (src/Debug.cpp:1524-1590). STAFF_SPELLS is the
# grant the staff has always made and the one the handler withholds, so its
# presence or absence is the whole measurement. Underscores render as spaces
# on screen, so the pattern the check greps for is "STAFF SPELLS from SS ITEM".

ESC     # dismiss the arrival-room description; now in play
w       # wizard mode
y       # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
w       # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
DOWN DOWN
SPACE   # Free Advancement: NO -> YES
ESC

d       # character sheet
# Each G is followed by every menu a mage level can raise, and the list is
# given TWICE because the menus do not always arrive in this order -- a
# fourth level brings the attribute prompt as well as the spell prompt, and
# @while is a single test against whatever is on screen when it is read, so a
# directive whose menu has not appeared yet does nothing and is spent. Two
# passes cover any order of two menus. A pass whose menu never appears costs
# nothing.
G
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
G
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
G
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
G
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@while "Learn which spell" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@expect "Mage 5"
@dump:sheet
ESC

# --- take a Staff of the Abyss and put it in the weapon hand ---
#   RIGHT*3      walks the acquisition's glyph row to Staves: ItemGlyphs
#                (src/Tables.cpp:320-323) puts -AI_STAFF three steps along.
#   @until       the candidate list has no menu letters, so the cursor is
#     "Staff of  walked. Three rows show and the MIDDLE one is selected, so
#     the Abyss" the @until stops one row EARLY, on the pass where the Abyss
#     DOWN       first appears as the row below Winter. The Abyss is the last
#   DOWN*3       staff entity in lib/m_items.irh and the list is in file
#                order, so there is no row after it and the extra DOWNs
#                saturate harmlessly. The check does not trust any of that:
#                it reads the staff's name back off the inventory screen.
#   ENTER 2 ENTER  take it, and answer "Enter Item Level:" with 2, which is
#                the plus the page states (src/Debug.cpp:604-620).
#   b SPACE s    the pickup drops the staff into the in-air slot and opens
#                Inventory Mode. 'b' is the Weapon Hand -- the slot lettering
#                skips 'd', which is the Drop key -- SPACE swaps the staff
#                into it (src/Managers.cpp:873), and 's' stows the starting
#                knife that swap pushed into the air, because the manager
#                will not close while anything is held there
#                (src/Managers.cpp:835-848).
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*3
@until "Staff of the Abyss" DOWN
DOWN*3
ENTER
2
ENTER
b
SPACE
s
@dump:staff-wielded
ESC

# --- what the wielder now holds ---
# The stati list is longer than the box, so it is walked in twelve-row steps.
# Four dumps cover a fifth-level mage's list with room to spare; the check
# greps all of them together.
w
@choose "Examine Player Data"
@until "Stati (" DOWN
@dump:stati-1
DOWN*12
@dump:stati-2
DOWN*12
@dump:stati-3
DOWN*12
@dump:stati-4
ESC
